WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:05.520
Hi everyone, welcome to Yale. My name is
Simone and I'm a senior majoring in

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.929
computer science. And I'm Sam. I'm a
junior double majoring in classics and

00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:14.429
political science. And we will be your
tour guides today. At Yale students pursue

00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:19.350
both the arts and the sciences, so in one
day you might do research at a

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:23.820
state-of-the-art lab with a Nobel Prize
winner and take a class at the Yale art gallery.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.490
Yalies embrace tradition and innovation,

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:30.840
so you might study with a
renowned Shakespeare scholar and perform

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.834
slam poetry at a cultural center.

00:00:32.834 --> 00:00:36.000
You might build a robot -
and sing with an a cappella group.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.000
Debate public policy - and
dance with Yale Jashan Bhangra.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.500
Excuse me. Are we gonna see all this on the tour?

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.000
We're certainly gonna try.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.500
Could we get a little background music? &lt;snaps&gt;

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:55.000
Uh...How about something with a beat?

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.700
Follow us.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.830
Yale offers more than 2,000 courses
every year, and students can choose from

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:15.700
over 80 majors. There's no core
curriculum, so we get to pick every one

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.270
of our courses ourselves, and we don't have to
choose a major until the end of our

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:23.110
second year. Classes come in all
sizes, but three quarters of them enroll

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:27.100
fewer than 20 students, so you might
choose to take a larger lecture course

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:28.000
like this:

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.100
Dean Chun: Together with Yale students, my

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:35.270
laboratory is developing techniques to
use brain imaging to read out the mind.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:39.439
We can show participants faces as shown here on the left and

00:01:39.439 --> 00:01:44.000
decode their brain activity patterns and guess, or reconstruct what they were seeing...

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Simone: or small discussion seminars like this:

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.220
Professor Biggs: So, what do we make of the fact that at the height of WW II, Winston Churchill

00:01:55.220 --> 00:02:00.360
pulled Laurence Olivier from
the Service to make a film of Henry V?

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:05.500
Well I think it supports the notion that
Henry V should be considered the GREAT

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:10.000
patriotic drama the English theatrical canon.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.300
... I'm inclined to agree with you,

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:18.100
but let's try the counter-argument: that Henry was a serious war-monger...

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.150
When doing
independent research, you'll have access

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:24.440
to some of the world's most expansive
libraries. This one library holds over 4

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:29.269
million books on 16 floors. Besides books, it also contains thousands of musical

00:02:29.269 --> 00:02:32.500
scores, maps, and newspapers from around
the world.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:39.170
In the Beinecke library you'll find
one of the world's largest collections

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:44.239
of rare books and manuscripts, including
ancient Egyptian papyrus, one of

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:48.860
Beethoven's original scores, and
manuscripts written by Langston Hughes.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:54.000
The marble walls filter sunlight to
preserve the books inside.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.180
Yale students learn by doing. With over 800
science and engineering labs, a 24/7

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.180
makerspace with state-of-the-art
equipment, students can push past the

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:05.780
boundaries of their scientific curiosity,
and innovate solutions to some of the

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.000
world's greatest challenges.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:12.600
Yale sponsors countless  research projects, both on campus and abroad.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.100
There are over a hundred paid fellowships reserved for

00:03:15.139 --> 00:03:18.590
first-year students in science and
engineering. That means you get access to

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:21.160
hands-on experience at a lab right away.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.860
&lt;explosion&gt;

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:29.560
There are countless opportunities to
learn outside the classroom. You might

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:34.670
learn about sustainable agriculture at
the Yale farm, or record an original music

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:39.590
composition in a professional studio. You
can learn a new foreign language abroad

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:44.590
on a Yale fellowship. You might even take
a class here at the Yale Art Gallery.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:46.700
Professor: Interesting use of color here.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.200
But no matter where your academic journey takes you,

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:53.900
you'll experience Yale's most
distinctive feature: the residential colleges.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:00.599
Every incoming student is randomly
assigned to one of 14 residential colleges.

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:03.569
The colleges have nothing to do
with your major. They're just smaller

00:04:03.569 --> 00:04:06.959
communities that reflect the diversity
of the student body. Each has its own

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:11.579
facilities, events and traditions. Besides
just housing students, the colleges are

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:16.320
also supportive communities that include
some of our most valued advisors.

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.920
My name is Jorge and I'm a first year
counselor. I was chosen by the college to

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:28.380
live among first-years and serve as a
senior advisor, helping with everything

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:32.160
from homesickness to course selection to
finding the laundry room.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:38.550
I'm Jessie Hill. I'm the Dean of this college. I help
students choose classes and explore

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:42.510
research and internship opportunities. I
support students through every academic

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.900
and personal challenge.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:48.900
And I'm Dr. Laurie Santos, the Head of this college. I shape

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.990
the cultural social and academic events
in the college and I ensure that each

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:55.380
student feels welcome. I make sure to get
to know every student in the college

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:56.900
one-on-one.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:58.800
Checkmate.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.900
&lt;claps&gt;

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.300
Inside every college are
awesome facilities like game rooms with

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.500
ping pong and pool tables.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.500
Common rooms where you can relax, hang out
and unwind with your friends. Play games -

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:23.040
&lt;music&gt;

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:27.500
Spaces like dance studios, woodworking
shops, music practice rooms,

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:28.700
and workout rooms.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:31.440
Girl: Come on Sam!  Come on! Lift that! Come on!

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:36.800
And, of course, when you're craving
that burger, milkshake, or other late night snack,

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.300
the butteries.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:42.060
&lt;music&gt;

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.300
But perhaps the
most important spot in any residential

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:48.360
college is the dining hall, where you'll
find great food and great friends.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.200
This college has the pizza oven where...

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:51.800
Sorry to interrupt. Can we hold for a sec?

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:52.800
&lt;snaps&gt;

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:57.500
That's better. OK, right about now you're
probably wondering:

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:00.500
How could I possibly afford to go to Yale?

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.960
Well the good news
is Yale's need-based financial aid meets

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:08.550
one hundred percent of your financial need,
with awards that won't require you and

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:13.800
your family to take out loans. If your
family's annual income is $65,000 a year

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:18.660
or less, then there is no parent
contribution at all. Awards are

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:22.440
calculated to include the full cost of
tuition, books, living on campus and

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:28.760
personal expenses. Yale even covers its
students to study abroad. My point is:

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:33.740
a Yale education is affordable.
For everyone!

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:43.830
Within the residential colleges students
live in suites. Bedrooms are attached to

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:49.180
common rooms, where you can study and
hang out. Suites range in size from three

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:53.520
to twelve students, and you usually share
a bathroom with your roommates, the bath...

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.660
&lt;scream&gt;

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:02.300
And, scene!
OK that's a wrap for rehearsal. Great

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:05.100
job today everyone. Can I talk to
wardrobe backstage?

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:09.400
You'll spend most of your time outside your suite though, participating is some of the

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:13.500
hundreds of student organizations at Yale like theater and singing.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:18.500
Singing: Woah - that's why we chose Yale

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:22.800
Make your voice heard in a political group, mock trial or debate.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:24.800
Debater: In today's
political climate, what we need is more..

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:26.900
Debater: I couldn't agree more!
Civil discourse!

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.200
You can join one of Yale's many dance groups.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:44.400
&lt;Music&gt;

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:48.700
And there are dozens of groups affiliated with Yale's four cultural centers.

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:56.230
I did not build this house, but I claim
every beam and bone as my own, because

00:07:56.230 --> 00:08:02.650
here I find people who will laugh with
and at me, but always out of love. I find

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:07.600
a community that shows up when I ask
them to and especially when I don't. With

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.900
every gathering, we build a home that
will last.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:12.560
&lt;snaps&gt;

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:16.920
The best part is that your activities don't have to have any connection to your major.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:19.320
You could join a music group without taking a single
music course.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:21.900
You might even start your own jazz combo.

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:31.860
&lt;music&gt;

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:36.820
Or you can play with the Yale Symphony
Orchestra.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:52.460
&lt;music&gt;

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:56.200
You can stay active with more than a hundred
different athletic teams.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:59.300
Including varsity, club, and intramural.

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:05.440
&lt;music&gt;

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:10.940
And we're just scratching the surface. Yale has
over 500 student groups!

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:13.540
If you have a passion, you can pursue it!

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:18.560
&lt;music&gt;

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:21.040
If you want to try something new, you can try it!

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:23.840
&lt;music&gt;

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:26.200
And if you have a dream, you can follow it!

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:32.400
&lt;music&gt;

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:34.300
Any questions?

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:36.300
&lt;music&gt;

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:38.780
How do I apply?!

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:47.780
&lt;quiet outdoor ambience&gt;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:49.980
You just go to the website.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:10:47.680
&lt;music&gt;

